\section{Write Three Contracts for One Operation}

A foreign function interface (FFI) does not make two languages agree.
It defines a place where their implementations must agree on selected
facts. Take a native image library whose entry point borrows a row of
pixels, applies a transform, and writes the number of pixels processed.
The machine contract specifies symbol, calling convention, widths,
alignment and argument placement. The value contract specifies what a
pixel is, whether a null pointer denotes an empty row and whether the
count is pixels or bytes. The protocol contract specifies who may call
the function, how long it may keep the pointer and what it has done
when it reports failure. A wrapper that gets just the first contract
right may run to completion and still return an incorrect image.

The survey extract on C APIs in extension languages describes the same
conversion and invocation tasks realized by different API designs:
Lua uses a virtual stack to mediate values, while other APIs in the
survey expose handles or descriptors through C variables. A native
wrapper should use the host's abstraction for accessing values, not
infer a heap layout from what a debugger happens to display. Furr and
Foster study this distinction directly: their analyses model how native
code accesses high-level values and track facts such as tags and offsets.
Ordinary C type checking does not prove that a particular host descriptor
contains the tag needed by an extraction operation.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{0.64\linewidth}}
\hline
Contract & Example obligation for a row transform \\
\hline
Machine & Count and output count use the library target's \texttt{size\_t}. \\
Value & Four bytes form one pixel in specified channel order. \\
Lifetime & The input read lease ends before the native call returns. \\
Effect & A failed operation does not publish a new interpreter image. \\
Thread & Only calls on the session owner thread may touch the transform state. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Make Failure and Aliasing Part of the Interface}

Suppose the API accepts a read-only input view and a mutable destination
view. It declares overlapping ranges invalid, says that the destination
may be partially modified on failure, and reports a processed count only
on success. The language wants a functional operation: its original
image remains unchanged and it yields either a complete new image or
an error. Use a fresh private destination and publish it only after
validating the returned count. This decision comes from the desired
language effect, not from the shape of the C signature. Using the input
as the destination to save one allocation would violate both layers.

With a moving collector, the input owner must be live and the exposed
address must not change during the borrow. A root is not automatically
a pin. The wrapper can copy the input into native storage if no
appropriate address-stability mechanism exists. It can also share a
stable allocation if the language guarantees the view will not be
mutated concurrently. This is why \Cref{fig:visual-385} separates the
raw entry point from the checks that make the public operation safe
for ordinary clients. The model assumes a conforming library; a thin
in-process binding cannot enforce arbitrary native memory accesses.

