\section{Turn the Obligations into a Call Plan}

\begin{verbatim}
transform_row(session, image, row):
    require_owner_thread(session)
    require(open(session))
    require(0 <= row < image.height)
    require(image.format == RGBA8)
    pixels := checked_c_size_t(image.width)
    bytes := checked_multiply(pixels, 4)
    view := image.checked_row(row, bytes)
    if pixels == 0:
        return empty_rgba_row()

    with read_lease_or_copy(view) as src:
        with allocate_native(bytes) as dst:
            written := native_size_t_cell()
            status := raw_transform(session.handle, src, dst,
                                    pixels, address(written))
            if status != OK:
                return translate(status)
            if read(written) != pixels:
                return ContractViolation
            return copy_to_new_row(dst, bytes)
\end{verbatim}

Range validation is not just a sign test. Converting a large width to
the native count type and then multiplying can truncate or overflow.
Validate the width before narrowing and compute the byte size with
checked arithmetic in the domain used for addressing. The row lookup
also checks the offset and extent against the actual allocation; a
correct row size does not prove a particular row lies within bounds.
The early return works only because the public operation defines empty
rows to have no native side effects. For a stateful transform that
advances a line counter even on empty input, that shortcut is wrong.

The output cell is read only on success because failure does not promise
to initialize it. The fresh destination may still be destroyed after
failure because deallocation is independent of initialization. Copying
assigns the new image its own storage; adopting the destination instead
would require transferring responsibility to an object that carries
the matching deallocator. The nested leases release in reverse order
on every normal or translated exit, including allocation failure after
the input is prepared.

\section{Test Conditions That Identical Values Hide}

Use a four-pixel row with distinct byte values in each channel and a
stride larger than its visible width. The distinct channels reveal a
channel-order mismatch; a stride with slack distinguishes a row lookup
from a naive multiplication by width alone. A test implementation can
write half the destination, return failure, and leave the output cell
untouched. Verify that it produces no host image, leaks no allocation,
and never reads that cell. Test empty rows separately from a width so
large that computing its byte size fails. The former can be a legitimate
no-op; the latter is always rejected before entry.

Record which obligations the language enforces and which are assumptions
on the supplier. Generated stubs can encode convention and sizes.
The wrapper can check values and arrange leases. Only the native
implementation, specification or a stronger isolation mechanism can
establish compliance with no retention and bounded writes. Test the
public postcondition, not just the status: returning the correct error
after exposing a partially transformed array is still an observable
violation. A useful review question is whether replacing the native
function with another implementation of the same published contract
could reveal any undocumented assumption in the wrapper.

\section{Distinguish a Retryable Call from a Retryable Result}

Suppose the transform additionally updates an internal exposure
estimate for the next row. A resource failure before entry permits
a retry without changing the estimate. A failure while copying the
native destination back into the host, after the native function
reported success, has a different meaning: the next row will see
the new estimate even though the caller never received an image.
The published-image guarantee does not imply rollback of the
session. To promise atomicity over both image and session, reserve
host result storage before entry and use an infallible commit,
or negotiate a native prepare and commit protocol. If neither is
available, expose a result that distinguishes not-applied from
applied-but-unavailable. Treating both cases as retryable silently
applies the same row twice.

Exercise these two paths with a fixture that increments a native
counter only at its commit point. Before-entry allocation failure
must leave the counter unchanged. Failure after native success
must either be impossible by construction or observable as a
different effect. This is an interface test even though neither
branch exercises a novel machine representation.
